.SH GETTING STARTED
One executable serves three roles: it captures this machine's audio and sends it out (server), it receives audio from another machine and plays it (client), or it just scans the network to see who is already streaming (discover). Without any flag wfas decides on its own: launched from a terminal it prints a short hint, launched by a double click it opens the desktop app.
.SS Entry point
.TP
.B (no flags)
From a terminal, print a short hint. Otherwise open the GUI.
.TP
.B \-\-gui
Open the desktop app.
.RS
.PP
Can be combined with a mode to open the window and start straight away, as in 'wfas \-\-gui \-\-mode server \-\-multicast'.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-cli
Stay in the terminal. Runs an audio server unless another mode is given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tray
Start the GUI without the Linux tray icon.
.RS
.PP
Use this if the app segfaults inside libgtk\-3 on launch; the tray is the only part that touches GTK. Same as WFAS_NO_TRAY=1, or 'wfas config set ui.linuxTray OFF' to make it permanent.
.RE
.SS Modes
.TP
.B \-\-server
Start as audio source. Shorthand for \-\-mode server.
.TP
.B \-\-client
Start as audio receiver. Shorthand for \-\-mode client.
.TP
.B \-\-mode <m>
server | client | discover
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
server    capture this machine's audio and serve it
client    receive audio from a server and play it
discover  scan the network for active servers, then exit
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SS Discovery
.PP
Discovery is passive: servers announce themselves on the multicast group, so nothing is probed or port\-scanned. Each entry reports the transport it was seen on and its address family.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep scanning and update the list live instead of exiting after one pass.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help network.
.SH SERVER OPTIONS
The server captures what this machine is playing and sends it out over the native WFAS protocol. Everything here is about that native stream; the extra protocols that can run alongside it live under 'Streaming protocols'.
.SS Ports and delivery
.TP
.B \-\-port <n>
WFAS streaming port.
.br
Default: 9090.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-port <n>
Port the microphone return channel comes back on.
.br
Default: 9092.
.TP
.B \-\-multicast
Serve to every listener on the group instead of one unicast peer.
.RS
.PP
Implied by \-\-rtp, \-\-http, \-\-dlna and \-\-snapcast. Note that \-\-auth\-mode applies to unicast only.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-interface <name>
Network interface to bind and announce on.
.br
Default: Auto.
.SS Capture engine
.TP
.B \-\-legacy\-engine
Use the legacy FFmpeg grabber instead of the native C audio engine.
.RS
.PP
The native engine is the default on all platforms:
.RS 2
.nf
  Windows  WASAPI loopback (no virtual driver needed)
  macOS    ScreenCaptureKit
  Linux    PulseAudio/PipeWire via dlopen
.fi
.RE
Use \-\-legacy\-engine on Linux if PulseAudio is unavailable, or for compatibility with older setups. Alias: \-\-no\-native\-engine.
.RE
.SS Session descriptor
.PP
An SDP file describes the stream well enough for VLC, ffplay or any RTP receiver to open it. Only meaningful together with \-\-rtp.
.TP
.B \-\-sdp
Print stream.sdp to stdout when the server starts.
.TP
.B \-\-sdp\-out <path>
Write stream.sdp to a file, for example /tmp/stream.sdp.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help protocols, wfas \-\-help security, wfas \-\-help network.
.SH CLIENT OPTIONS
The client receives a WFAS stream and plays it on a local device. With no \-\-connect it discovers a server by itself and joins the first one it sees.
.SS Connection and output
.TP
.B \-\-connect <ip>
Server address to connect to. Implies client mode.
.br
Default: auto\-discover.
.TP
.B \-\-output <name>
Audio output device name.
.br
Default: system default.
.RS
.PP
Matching is partial and case\-insensitive, so a fragment such as 'Loopback' is enough. Run 'wfas devices' to see the exact names this system reports.
.RE
.SS Playback
.TP
.B \-\-volume <0\-100>
Initial volume percentage.
.br
Default: 100.
.TP
.B \-\-mute
Start muted.
.TP
.B \-\-latency <ms>
Jitter buffer used on the Wi\-Fi link.
.br
Default: 120, range 0\-5000.
.RS
.PP
Larger values survive a noisier network, smaller ones cut the delay. The USB buffer is separate and is set by \-\-usb\-latency. Persists as audio.latencyMs when saved with 'wfas config set'.
.RE
.SS Microphone return channel
.PP
Sends the client's microphone back to the server (talkback), independent of the main server\-to\-client audio stream.
.TP
.B \-\-mic
Enable the microphone return channel.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-input <name>
Microphone device name. Same partial matching as \-\-output.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-routing <m>
mix | virtual | off
.br
Default: mix when \-\-mic is given.
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
mix      blend the mic into the server's captured audio
virtual  expose the mic on the server as a virtual device
off      disable
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SS Audio devices  (wfas devices)
.PP
Lists every audio device Java Sound reports on this system, with the exact name to hand to \-\-output or \-\-mic\-input, what each one can do, and why any of them was skipped. Alias: list\-devices. Add \-\-json for machine\-readable output.
.TP
.B devices
List the audio devices this system reports.
.PP
A device is normally kept only if it answers a format probe. That probe opens the card, so it fails whenever another audio server (PulseAudio, PipeWire, JACK) holds it, which is common on Linux and on Raspberry Pi. When no device passes, wfas keeps the full list instead of leaving you with nothing, and 'devices' says so explicitly.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help runtime, wfas \-\-help network, wfas \-\-help config.
.SH STREAMING PROTOCOLS
Besides its own protocol the server can speak four standard ones at the same time, so receivers that have never heard of WFAS can still play the audio. Each of these implies \-\-multicast. None of them is authenticated or encrypted: \-\-auth\-mode, \-\-auth\-key and \-\-encrypt cover the native WFAS stream only.
.SS RTP
.PP
Plain RTP for VLC, ffplay and hardware receivers. Pair it with \-\-sdp so the receiver knows what it is getting.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp
Enable the RTP protocol. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-port <n>
RTP port.
.br
Default: 9094.
.SS HTTP
.PP
A plain HTTP stream any browser or media player can open by URL.
.TP
.B \-\-http
Enable the HTTP stream. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-port <n>
HTTP port.
.br
Default: 8080.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-safari
Serve Safari\-compatible AAC. Implies \-\-http.
.SS DLNA
.PP
Pushes the audio to the DLNA renderers saved in the settings, so the renderer starts playing without anyone touching it.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna
Push audio to the saved DLNA renderers. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-port <n>
DLNA media endpoint port.
.br
Default: 8081.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-format <f>
auto | lpcm | wav | mp3 | adts
.br
Default: auto.
.SS Snapcast
.PP
Acts as a Snapcast server for synchronised multiroom audio. Any snapclient on the network (Raspberry Pi, ESP32, Home Assistant, the Snapcast mobile apps) can join and stay in sync.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast
Act as a Snapcast server. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-port <n>
Snapcast audio stream port.
.br
Default: 1704.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-control\-port <n>
Snapcast JSON\-RPC control port.
.br
Default: 1705.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-codec <c>
pcm | flac | opus
.br
Default: pcm.
.RS
.PP
flac roughly halves the bandwidth; opus needs 48000:16:2.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-chunk <n>
Chunk size in ms: 10 | 20 | 40 | 60
.br
Default: 20.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-buffer <n>
Client playback buffer in ms.
.br
Default: 1000.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-name <s>
Stream identifier advertised to clients.
.br
Default: default.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help security, wfas \-\-help runtime.
.SH USB & NETWORK
How the audio physically gets across: over Wi\-Fi, over the USB cable, or both, and which address family to use.
.SS USB link
.TP
.B \-\-usb
Stream over the USB cable instead of Wi\-Fi.
.RS
.PP
Enable USB tethering on the Android phone: the phone becomes the gateway and the app finds the link on its own. Lower and far steadier jitter than Wi\-Fi, which is what lets the buffer shrink. Not available on macOS: there is no built\-in RNDIS driver, so Android USB tethering does not come up. Overrides the saved net.usbMode for this run.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-no\-usb
Force the USB link off even if it is enabled in the config.
.TP
.B \-\-usb\-latency <ms>
Jitter buffer used only while the USB link is up. Implies \-\-usb.
.br
Default: 20, range 5\-120.
.RS
.PP
The Wi\-Fi buffer set by \-\-latency is left untouched.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-usb\-iface <name>
Force a specific tethering interface. Implies \-\-usb.
.RS
.PP
Accepts the interface name or its display name as listed by \-\-debug, or 'Auto' to restore automatic detection.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-wfas\-mode <m>
always | not\-on\-usb | off
.br
Default: not\-on\-usb.
.RS
.PP
When this device serves the native WFAS protocol:
.RS 2
.nf
  always      always, over Wi\-Fi and over the cable
  not\-on\-usb  over Wi\-Fi, but suppressed as soon as the USB link
              comes up
  off         never over Wi\-Fi; only the cable and the other
              protocols remain
.fi
.RE
With 'off' and no \-\-rtp/\-\-http and no USB link there is nothing left to serve, and the server refuses to start.
.RE
.SS Interface and address family
.TP
.B \-\-interface <name>
Network interface to bind and announce on.
.br
Default: Auto.
.TP
.B \-\-ip4, \-\-ipv4
IPv4 only: bind 0.0.0.0, announce and listen on 239.255.0.1 alone.
.TP
.B \-\-ip6, \-\-ipv6
IPv6 only: the [ff02::5746] group alone.
.PP
Without either flag the app is dual\-stack: it binds the wildcard, joins both groups, and picks per peer the most usable address, where a routable v4 or v6 beats a link\-local. Force a family only to work around a broken network; on a v6\-only LAN auto already does the right thing.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help config.
.SH SECURITY & PAIRING
Everything in this section applies to the native WFAS protocol only. RTP, HTTP, DLNA and Snapcast are standard protocols: their clients are never authenticated and their audio always goes out in the clear, so enabling them alongside \-\-encrypt exposes the very audio you are encrypting.
.SS Authorization and encryption
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-mode <m>
off | ask | key
.br
Default: off, unicast only.
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
off  accept anyone
ask  prompt on the terminal for each client that connects
key  require the pre\-shared key
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-key <key>
Pre\-shared key. Implies \-\-auth\-mode key.
.RS
.PP
The key is never sent on the wire: both sides prove they hold it through a mutual HMAC challenge\-response.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt
Encrypt the audio with ChaCha20\-Poly1305.
.RS
.PP
Requires a key, so pair it with \-\-auth\-key, or use \-\-qr to have one generated. See 'wfas \-\-protocol' for the wire details.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-qr
Generate a key, start encrypted, and print the pairing QR.
.RS
.PP
Server mode. Implies \-\-auth\-mode key and \-\-encrypt, so it needs no \-\-auth\-key: the code carries the key it just made. While the server runs, p prints a new invite and r rotates the key.
.RE
.SS QR pairing  (wfas pair <command>)
.PP
Hands a receiver everything it needs in one shot: address, port and a freshly generated 256\-bit key. The key is never typed and never travels on the wire; the invite carries it and the handshake proves it. Invites last 120 seconds.
.TP
.B pair invite
Generate an invite and draw it as a QR code on the terminal.
.RS
.PP
With a server already running, the invite is generated by that instance so it matches the live session; otherwise it is generated offline and saved to the config, ready for the next 'wfas \-\-server'. Add \-\-watch for a live countdown that renews the code on expiry: press n for a new invite, r for a new key, k to reveal the key, q to quit.
.RE
.TP
.B pair regenerate
Same, but always with a brand new key. Aliases: rekey, new\-key.
.RS
.PP
In multicast this evicts every listener still using the old key, which is exactly what you want after handing the code to the wrong person.
.RE
.TP
.B pair connect <link>
Join using an invite link. Alias: join.
.RS
.PP
Accepts the wifiaudio://pair?... form or the https one. Starts the client with the key already applied.
.RE
.TP
.B pair inspect <link>
Decode a link and show what it contains, without connecting.
.RS
.PP
Exits non\-zero if the invite has expired. Aliases: check, parse. Also available as 'wfas inspect <link>'.
.RE
.TP
.B pair encode <text>
Render any text as a QR code on the terminal. Alias: render.
.RS
.PP
Also available as 'wfas encode <text>'.
.RE
.TP
.B pair status
Show pairing state, key origin and handler registration.
.TP
.B pair off
Turn QR pairing off and restore the manually typed key. Alias: disable.
.TP
.B pair register
Force\-register the wifiaudio:// handler with this OS.
.RS
.PP
Per\-user, no administrator rights. Every run already does this in the background, rewriting the entry when it points at a different executable, so moving or reinstalling the app repairs itself. Use this only to see the repair fail loudly.
.RE
.TP
.B pair unregister
Remove the wifiaudio:// handler.
.SS QR rendering
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qr
Print only the link, no ASCII art.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Two characters per module instead of half\-blocks.
.RS
.PP
Use it if the terminal font has no U+2580/U+2584. Needs twice the width.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-invert
Invert the code for terminals with a light background.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-key
Print the pairing key instead of masking it.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help protocols, wfas \-\-help reference.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Persistent settings live in a single config.json shared by the CLI and the GUI. Changes apply the next time a server or client starts, or the next time the GUI opens.
.SS Settings  (wfas config <command>)
.TP
.B config list
Show every setting and its current value. Aliases: ls, show.
.TP
.B config get <key>
Print one setting, for example audio.sampleRate.
.TP
.B config set <key> <value>
Change one setting and save it.
.TP
.B config path
Print the config.json path for this system. Alias: where.
.TP
.B config edit
Open config.json in your default editor. Alias: open.
.TP
.B config reset
Restore all settings to their defaults.
.TP
.B config export [file]
Write the config to a file, or to stdout if omitted. Alias: save.
.TP
.B config import <file>
Load a config.json and make it active. Alias: load.
.PP
Add \-\-json to any config command for machine\-readable output.
.SS Overrides
.TP
.B \-\-config <path>
Use an alternate settings file for this run.
.SS Firewall  (wfas firewall <command>)   [Windows only]
.PP
Opens the inbound UDP ports so clients can reach this machine, exactly like the button in the GUI settings. Prompts once for administrator approval. Alias: fw.
.TP
.B firewall allow [ports]
Allow inbound UDP.
.RS
.PP
With no ports, opens the configured streaming, discovery (9091) and mic ports. Or pass a list, as in 'wfas firewall allow 9090,9091'.
.RE
.TP
.B firewall status
Show whether the WFAS firewall rule is active.
.PP
Add \-\-json for machine\-readable output.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help network, wfas \-\-help reference.
.SH RUNTIME & MONITORING
Talk to an instance that is already running, watch what it is doing, or look at the audio itself.
.SS Runtime control  (wfas control <command>)
.PP
Sent over a local IPC socket to the running instance, GUI or CLI alike.
.TP
.B control volume <0\-100>
Set the output volume.
.TP
.B control mute | unmute
Toggle audio output.
.TP
.B control stop
Stop the running instance.
.TP
.B control status
Show the current streaming status.
.RS
.PP
Includes the RTP, HTTP, DLNA and Snapcast side\-protocols and their connected clients.
.RE
.SS Visualizer
.TP
.B \-\-viz [theme]
Animated ASCII spectrum histogram of the audio stream.
.RS
.PP
Optional theme: a hex color such as #1e88e5 recolors the whole view via the Material You palette, or 'rainbow' for an animated dynamic rainbow.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-groove [amount]
Only with \-\-viz: adaptive spectrum that follows the melody.
.RS
.PP
Instead of drawing raw levels, where bass pins the low bars at full scale and everything else flattens into one blob, each band is compared with its frequency neighbours, so a note that pokes out of its region is lifted, and a slow per\-band envelope is subtracted, so the constant part of the mix stops dominating. Optional amount: soft | normal | hard, or 0\-160. Press g in the visualizer to toggle it live.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-monitor
Only with \-\-viz: no server, just visualize the system audio. Alias: \-\-listen.
.RS
.PP
Reads the loopback without lowering the system volume.
.RE
.SS Diagnostics and output
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Live debug HUD, then internal logs.
.RS
.PP
Audio packet table plus, with \-\-mic, the microphone send/receive table. Also lists the network interfaces by name and display name, which is what \-\-usb\-iface expects.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit all output as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress logs; only errors go to stderr.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help config.
